WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.110
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:04.810
We're currently being visited by a friend
and his 6-year-old daughter and I asked her

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:09.679
what she would like me to make my video about
today, and she said, "tarantulas in the guacamole."

00:00:09.679 --> 00:00:14.360
I'm gonna assume that she means "Let's talk
about big, stupid, irrational fears".

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.860
It's probably not what she meant, but it feels
like it might be a bit of a stretch to spend

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:21.130
the entire video talking about guacamole tarantulas.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:26.010
So instead, I want to talk about what I think
is the most common irrational fear in America

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.010
today.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:31.980
And that is the fear that 50% of Americans
who surround us are thoughtless, and terrible,

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:32.980
and foolish.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:37.160
Now this fear is every bit as irrational as
thinking there might be tarantulas in the

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.001
guacamole, but we all seem to believe it.

00:00:39.001 --> 00:00:43.260
And I think that this comes from our addiction,
our absolute addiction to outrage.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:48.120
Now, I wanna be clear, I'm not talking about
anger at systems of oppression, or frustration

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.470
at disenfranchisement, or fear that we are
making intractable decisions that are going

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:55.870
to have dramatic and negative consequences
for future generations.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:58.680
Those are all completely rational reasons
to be upset.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:05.040
I'm talking about the continuous and unrelenting
cycles of manufactured outrage, really manufactured

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:09.580
fear that I, you know, used to be a part of
and I used to encourage.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:13.630
I used to think that outrage was our most
powerful tool for positive change.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:17.670
You had to get people angry, and you had to
get 'em up and moving and doing stuff.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:23.270
But what I've seen in my 35 years is that
angry people, the stuff they do, is mostly

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:27.690
they talk to each other about how angry they
are, and they talk to each other about how

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.180
wrong other people are, and then every once
in a while they interact with those wrong

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.920
people and then they just are mean.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.090
And everyone does this.

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:38.920
Fake statistics get created and passed around,
we burn down straw men and we get to feel

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:44.369
good about it, because we believe, we've convinced
ourselves that this hatred - and let's be

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:48.270
honest, it's hatred - is somehow, like, leading
to positive change.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:53.070
But when manufactured outrage is the fuel
with which we fight, we enter into a kind

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:58.270
of outrage arms race where the winner of the
election is the one that can terrify its base

00:01:58.270 --> 00:01:59.270
the most.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.950
Now, as I've tried to put my own addiction
to outrage in check, I've become a lot more

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:08.280
sensitive to the people who are out there
pedalling in it, and those people, on both

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.750
sides, have started to make me feel like super
sick to my stomach, already.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:19.450
So as we enter - far too early - into the
2016 presidential season, I have already started

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:23.980
to figure out ways to build up my armor against
the outrage cycle.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:27.160
This doesn't just calm me down and, like,
make it possible for me to get up in the morning

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:31.840
without being, like, weighed down by all of
the tremendous fear, it also - and I truly

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:37.420
believe this - gives me a more accurate understanding
of the world and our country and our culture.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:43.040
Because outrage blinds us, it is built on
and builds bias and stereotypes.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:48.200
It makes people really bad at understanding
how society and other people actually function.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.270
So my tools, the ones I'm building, my armor
against the outrage cycle, are awareness of

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:58.819
my own cognitive biases, a belief in the fundamental
goodness of other people, that they want good

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:03.040
things for themselves and for their families,
and compassion.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.220
Those are the things I'm using, if you have
strategies for dealing with this, please let's

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.390
talk about that in the comments.

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:13.280
In the meantime, I just analyzed the whole
Nerdfighteria Census of 2015 over at Hankschannel

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:15.270
and the results were fascinating.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:19.560
Nerdfighteria has gotten older by three years
in the last year, which is weird.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:23.680
We've gone from 24% college graduates to 33%
college graduates!

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.860
People love Crash Course, which is exciting
and the vast majority of respondents have

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:30.300
suggested Crash Course to their friends or
people in their family.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:34.790
And people really want us to do some kind
of news show, which after having talked about

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:42.879
the current state of "news media," for the
last few minutes, I'm feeling compelled to

00:03:42.879 --> 00:03:43.879
do also.

00:03:43.879 --> 00:03:46.800
But it's very hard and I don't know that we'll
be able to do it, but we're looking into it,

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.190
we'll try to do that thing.

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:50.530
So thanks I'm glad that you would be interested
in that.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:51.569
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

